\section{Cost Model}

The cost of one inference is the sum of a fixed part and a part that
grows with the number of tokens. The fixed part covers the load of the
weights into the device memory.

\begin{displaymath}
C = c_{\text{load}} + n \cdot c_{\text{token}}
\end{displaymath}

The load cost is paid once per batch, so a large batch amortizes it. The
per-token cost is the same for every request of the same shape.

For a batch of one the load cost dominates below about two hundred
tokens. Above that point the per-token cost dominates, and the total
cost grows in a straight line with the token count.

\section{Column Layout}

The table uses a fixed width column and suppresses the pad on both
edges, so the rule lines up with the margin.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lp{4cm}r@{}}
Key & Description & Count \\[2ex]
\texttt{warm}  & Pages already resident in the device memory & 128 \\
\texttt{cold}  & Pages fetched on the first access & 64 \\[1ex]
\texttt{evict} & Pages returned to the host before the end & 12 \\
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The count column holds the median over ten runs. The displayed form of
the hit rate is
$$ h = \left. \frac{\mbox{warm}}{\mbox{warm} + \mbox{cold}} \right) $$
where the half open delimiter marks an open interval on the right.

The set of valid rates is $\{h \mid 0 \le h \le 1\}$.
